# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HireSnap — superproject ignore rules
#
# SCOPE WARNING, and the reason this file is short.
# `backend/` and `frontend/` are git submodules. Git sees a submodule as a single
# gitlink — ONE commit hash — and never descends into it. No pattern in this file
# applies to a single path inside a submodule; `git check-ignore` proves it:
# `backend/venv/x` is NOT ignored by this file. Each service's own `.gitignore`
# owns its artifacts (backend/.gitignore line 2 `/venv/`, frontend/.gitignore
# line 4 `/node_modules`).
#
# Mirrored from R:\GPTHub\.gitignore:3-8, where 252 lines of dead `frontend/...`
# and `/backend/...` rules silently matched nothing for the same reason.
# WHY the rule exists: a rule that cannot fire is worse than no rule — it reads
# like protection while the file it names is committed on the next `git add .`.
#
# The rules below therefore cover exactly two things:
#   1. paths the SUPERPROJECT itself owns (docker/, infra/, scripts/, docs/), and
#   2. paths that exist BEFORE submodule adoption, when backend/ and frontend/
#      are still plain directories and this file is the only ignore layer.
# Keep it that way: anything service-internal belongs in the service repo.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Secrets. The only block where a mistake is a leak, not a mess. ───────────
# Real values for dev and prod live beside the compose files and stay untracked.
# docker/.env.example is the ONLY committed environment manifest; everything
# else that starts with .env is a local copy holding real credentials.
# Mirrors R:\GPTHub\.gitignore:20-23.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
!.env.example

# ── Live stack data (bind mounts and named-volume backups), NOT cache ───────
# These directories ARE the running stack's state: uploaded videos and .npy
# embeddings under DATA_PATH, issued JWT/TLS material, model weights, and the
# application log files. `rm -rf` on any of them destroys local data that exists
# nowhere else — there is no migrations path to rebuild it (docs/KNOWN_GAPS.md).
# They are ignored because they must never be committed, not because they are
# disposable.
#
# The MySQL datadir itself is a NAMED VOLUME (docker volume `mysql_data`), not a
# bind mount, because bind-mounting a MySQL datadir on Windows is a corruption
# and performance hazard. The /infra/mysql_data/ rule below is therefore a
# RESERVED GUARD: it only fires if someone switches the database to a bind mount.
#
# Anchored per directory so a future top-level `storage/` or `data/` source
# folder is not swallowed by an over-broad pattern. scripts/check_no_generated_state.py
# fails the build when any of these has staged content.
/infra/storage/
/infra/mysql_data/
/infra/certs/
/infra/models/
/infra/logs/
/infra/nginx/certs/
/docker/.run-state/

# Why each of the three extra guards exists:
#   /infra/certs/   holds jwt-private.pem — committing it lets anyone mint valid
#                   access tokens against this deployment (docs/SECRETS.md).
#   /infra/models/  holds multi-GB, licence-bound weights and tokenizer
#                   checkpoints, mounted read-only; they are fetched, not authored.
#   /infra/logs/    holds runtime log output, which can quote request data and
#                   interpolated configuration.

# Runtime data roots. `--data` / DATA_PATH points inside these; the backend
# writes uploads and embeddings here at request time.
/data/
/logs/
*.log
# Local server cache written by the pipeline (backend/.gitignore:7 names it too,
# for the pre-adoption case).
/urls.txt

# Model weights and the vendored Cosmos tokenizer checkpoints. Multi-GB,
# licence-bound, and mounted read-only into the ml sidecar — never source.
#
# SCOPE. The entries below cover the superproject's own trees - and that is
# enough for the mounted material, because infra/models/ is protected by the
# /infra/models/ rule above. They cannot reach inside a service checkout, since a
# gitlink records one commit and stages no file; the authoritative lists there are
# backend/.gitignore and ml/.gitignore. A pattern that only looks like a guard is
# worse than none, so this says which one actually fires.
src/weights
pretrained_ckpts
**/pretrained_ckpts/

# ── Secrets material that must be generated on the host ─────────────────────
# The backend reads the RSA pair through JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH / JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PATH
# (docker/.env.example section 3), which the stack mounts from infra/certs into
# /app/secrets/certs. Any keypair generated IN PLACE inside a service checkout -
# the shape the fallback path (service/certs, derived from
# service/models/auth_models.py's __file__) would create - must never be committed:
# it would let anyone mint valid access tokens against this deployment. The pair
# is generated on the host (docs/SECRETS.md).
src/certs
**/src/certs/
service/certs
**/service/certs/

# ── Build output and dependencies, root level only ──────────────────────────
node_modules/
build/
dist/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# ── Tool state and OS noise ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.tmp-*.txt` are scratch dumps written by tooling in the repository root. They
# contain conversation or log text, are never inputs to a build, and are large
# enough to be committed by accident on a `git add .`.
.tmp-*
tmp-*
*.tmp
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
